// ===================== UNIT CHECK =====================
out.push(pb());
out.push(stage('✓', 'Unit 12 check'));
out.push(p('**A  Language — the answers.**'));
out.push(table(['**Q**', '1', '2', '3', '4', '5', '6', '7', '8'], [['Answer', 'b', 'c', 'b', 'c', 'a', 'b', 'b', 'a']], [1.2, 0.6, 0.6, 0.6, 0.6, 0.6, 0.6, 0.6, 0.6]));
out.push(p('⚠ **Q1 is the past participle after *was***, **and *was took* is the error to expect from learners who have the two past forms the wrong way round.** ⚠ **Q4 is *successor*, the person, against *succession*, the process** — **and a decision binds a person and not a process.** ⚠ **Q6 is *without* + gerund, which is the construction of the whole mediation:** *minuted without reopening the decision*. ⚠ **Q7 is *would*, the hypothetical, which is the grammar of a trigger** — **and a learner who writes *will* has turned a condition into a prediction.**'));
out.push(p('**B  Listening (Track 12.15).**   **The decision and its basis:** ⚠ **clause 19 accepted as drafted, on the basis that in a fact dispute the Company\'s evidence is better suited to arbitration than to a court.**   **What the dissenter asks for, in three parts:** ⚠ **that his dissent is minuted, with its reason, and that the decision is not reopened.**   **His reason, in one sentence:** ⚠ **that the dispute he expects is a money dispute and not a fact dispute, and that Beirut costs four times as much to decide a question about invoices.**   **Who wrote that case first:** ⚠ **Nour, on page 3a** — **and better than he would have.**   **What the chairman says a minuted dissent does:** ⚠ **it makes the decision one that was taken rather than assumed, and in five years the two sentences together say what nothing else can.**   **The dissenter\'s last line:** ⚠ **that if the trigger fires, his dissent becomes the recommendation without anybody having to reargue it.**'));
out.push(p('**C  Reading.**  ⚠ **The four lines: the decision · the basis · the review date, with an owner · the trigger, with an owner.** ⚠ **The trigger has two owners because it has two halves and no single person can see both** — **Mr Sayegh will know of a settlement before anybody, and Nour will know about the firm\'s own filing before he does.** ⚠ **A trigger with one owner who can see half of it fires once.** **Give the marks for the two halves; do not give them for *because two people are involved*.**'));
out.push(p('**D  Writing – sample (96 words).**'));
out.push(sample(null, [
  '⚠ **Do we hire from Shaheen\'s for March at the February rate, or go to tender and accept that nobody covers the first three Mondays?**',
  '⚠ **What decides it: no tender can produce a van before the 20th, and the two Monday-only customers take goods on the 2nd.**',
  '⚠ **The record. Decision: Shaheen\'s are instructed for March at the February rate. Basis: as above. Review: 1 November, before next year\'s tender is prepared; owner L. Barakat. Trigger: a tendered van available for a March start; owner Fadi Saeed, who has been told.**',
]));
out.push(box('Marking the unit check', [
  '**A  Language — 8 marks**, one each.',
  '**B  Listening — 6 marks**, one for each gap. ⚠ **Give the fourth mark only for both halves** — **that Nour wrote it AND that she wrote it better than he would have.** **The second half is the point of 12.4 and is what makes the mediation four minutes long.**',
  '**C  Reading — 5 marks:** the four lines (2) · ⚠ **the two halves of the trigger (2)** · ⚠ **and that a one-owner trigger fires once (1).**',
  '**D  Writing — 6 marks:** a question with two answers and a price inside it (2) · ⚠ **a deciding factor, singular (2)** · ⚠ **a record with a review date AND a trigger, each with an owner (2)** — **and no marks for the last two if either owner is missing.**',
  '**Total 25.** ⚠ **The profile to expect: A high, B high, C split, D high.** **C fails where a learner names the four lines and then explains the two owners as a courtesy to two departments**, ⚠ **which misses the mechanism.** **D is usually the strongest writing task in Book 1, because by Unit 12 every element of it has been drilled separately.**',
], C.lteal, C.navy));
out.push(note('Teacher\'s note. Four faults to watch, and the remedy for each. First, the decision written as a subject. "What should we do about X" can be discussed for an hour and leave the room where it found it, and the answer that gets taken is whichever one somebody says last. Insist on one question, two answers, and the price of the second inside it — and apply the availability test to any third, because an answer that cannot be had this month at a price the firm would pay is a preference and belongs in the options-not-taken list. Second, the list of reasons. Learners believe more reasons are safer and the arithmetic is the other way: a list is a menu and a reader orders the dish he can send back. Drill the test — would the answer change if this changed? — and insist on the direction as well, because in about a quarter of cases one item in a list of supporting reasons is not supporting at all. Third, the case against written badly in good faith. The remedy is to write it from the mouth of a named person who holds it, which forces the strong version because that person might read it; and the argument that persuades a class is the ranking of conclusions, which is that one-sidedness can be discounted by a reader and not having seen the argument cannot. Fourth, and this is the one that matters most after the course ends: the basis left out of the operative sentence. Ask Mr Ayoub\'s question of every paper for the rest of the term — which sentence will I be quoted in five years? — and then make them put the deciding factor inside it. Watch for "in the light of all the circumstances", "having considered" and "on balance", all three of which stand where a clause the writer can already say should be. Two notes on 12.7. The mediation is short because the case against had been written properly, and that connection should be made explicitly: Bassel asks only to be recorded because the argument he holds is already in the paper, in better words than his own, so there is nothing left to argue. And his last line — that if the trigger fires his dissent becomes the recommendation without being reargued — is the single most useful sentence in Book 1 for whoever is in that room in 2027, because it converts a review from a blank page into a drafted answer. It is worth asking a class whether they have ever seen a dissent recorded anywhere in their own workplace. The answer is usually no, and it explains why so many decisions in their own files cannot be told apart from habits.'));

// ===================== END OF BOOK 1: THE WORD LIST =====================
